    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is an enharmonic relationship?\n\nOptions: \nA. ♯G minor and B major\nB. E minor and ♯D minor\nC. F Mixolydian and F Dorian\nD. C Lydian and G Dorian\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Enharmonic relationships occur when two notes or keys sound the same but are spelled differently (e.g., C♯ and D♭). Among the options, E minor and ♯D minor both refer to the same pitch class set (E minor = E–F♯–G–A–B–C–D; ♯D minor = D♯–E♯–F♯–G♯–A♯–B–C♯), which enharmonically equate to E minor (since D♯ = E, E♯ = F♯, G♯ = A♭, A♯ = B♭, C♯ = D♭—but standard spelling of E minor uses natural notes except F♯ and C♯). However, strictly speaking, **E minor and ♯D minor** are not standard key names—♯D minor would be D♯ minor, which *enharmonically equals* E minor (since D♯ = E, though key signatures differ). This is the closest match.  \n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
