nd. Option A says “major second”, D says “major second” too — wait A says “Its inverted equivalent interval is a major second”, D says “The inverted property is a major second”. Both seem same. But check solfege: B says ♯C–♭E — that’s the inverted interval (from top to bottom), yes, but solfege might be La–Mi♭ or something. However standard answer: augmented sixth invert to minor seventh? No. Let’s recompute: augmented sixth = 8 semitones. Inversion = 12 – 8 = 4 semitones (since circle of 12). 4 semitones = perfect fourth? No, 5 semitones = P4, 4 = major third? Wait: major third = 4 semitones (C–E). Yes! So inverted interval is major third ascending. But earlier we got 3 semitones. Conflict arises from enharmonics. ♭E to ♯C: ♭E = D♯, so D♯–♯C = D♯–C♯ = descending major third, but ascending ♯C–D♯ = major third (4 semitones). Yes! So ♭E = D♯, so ♭E–♯C = D♯–C♯, which ascending is C♯–D♯ = major second? No: C♯–D♯ is whole step = 2 semitones. Wait: D♯ to C♯ is descending major third (3 semitones down). Ascending C♯–D♯ = 2 semitones. This is messy. Standard fact: augmented sixth interval inverts to minor third. Because augmented sixth = 8 semitones; minor third = 3 semitones; 8 + 3 = 11 ≠ 12. Hmm. Actually interval inversion: size + inversion = 9 for simple intervals (e.g., m3 = 3, inversion = M6 = 6, 3+6=9). Augmented sixth is compound? No, sixth = 9 semitones for major sixth, augmented sixth = 10 semitones? Wait: major sixth = 9 semitones (C–A). Augmented sixth = 10 semitones (C–A♯). So augmented sixth = 10 semitones. Inversion = 12 – 10 = 2 semitones = major second. Yes! So inverted interval is major second. Options A and D both say major second, but A says “Its inverted equivalent interval is a major second” — correct phrasing. D says “The inverted property is a major second” — ambiguous. Likely A is best. So answer is A.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
