    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "First, B𝄪 (B-double-sharp) enharmonically equals C natural. So the interval becomes C–?.\n\nOption A: C–♭G = C to G♭. That’s a diminished fifth (tritone), since C to G is a perfect fifth (7 semitones), and lowering G by one semitone makes it 6 semitones — yes, diminished fifth.\n\nOptions B and C are identical (\"♭C–♭G is a perfect fifth\") — likely a typo, but ♭C = B♭, so B♭–♭G = B♭ to G♭. That’s a diminished fifth as well (B♭ to F is perfect fourth; B♭ to G♭ is 6 semitones down from B♭ to G, i.e., diminished sixth? Wait—let’s compute: B♭ to G♭ = 8 semitones up? B♭ → C → D → E → F → G♭ = 6 semitones? Actually: B♭ (0), C (2), D (4), E (6), F (7), G♭ (8). Wait, G♭ is 8 semitones above B♭? No: B♭ to G♭ = 8 semitones (B♭–C=2, C–D=2, D–E=2, E–F=1, F–G♭=1 ⇒ total 8). Perfect fifth = 7 semitones, so 8 is an augmented fourth/diminished fifth? No, 8 semitones is an augmented fourth (e.g., C–F♯), but inverted it’s a diminished fifth (F♯–C). B♭–G♭ = minor sixth? Let's use standard: minor sixth = 8 semitones, yes — B♭ to G♭ is a minor sixth, not a fifth.\n\nSo A is correct: C–♭G = diminished fifth.\n\nBut B/C claim ♭C–♭G = perfect fifth — false, as shown.\n\nThus, A is correct.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{A}\n$$",
