    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_203",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is a different enharmonic interval from the double-augmented fifth?\n\nOptions: \nA. Major sixth\nB. Diminished seventh\nC. Double-augmented fourth\nD. Major sixth and diminished seventh\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A double-augmented fifth spans 15 semitones (a perfect fifth = 7, augmented once = 8, double-augmented = 9? Wait—no: a perfect fifth is 7 semitones; augmenting it once gives an augmented fifth (8 semitones); double-augmenting adds two semitones beyond perfect? Actually, augmentation adds one chromatic semitone per *augmentation* to the interval type, but strictly: an augmented fifth is 8 semitones, double-augmented fifth is 9 semitones—wait no: interval quality is defined relative to major/minor/perfect sizes. A perfect fifth is 7 semitones. Augmenting it once makes it 8 semitones (augmented fifth), double-augmenting adds another semitone → 9 semitones. However, 9 semitones is a major sixth (which is 9 semitones). So double-augmented fifth = 9 semitones = major sixth enharmonically. Diminished seventh is 8 semitones (minor seventh = 10, diminished = subtract 2 = 8), so not same. Double-augmented fourth: perfect fourth = 5 semitones; double-augment adds 2 → 7 semitones (same as perfect fifth), not 9. So only major sixth matches (9 semitones). But option D says “Major sixth and diminished seventh”—diminished seventh is 8 semitones (e.g., C→B♭♭ = 8 semitones), so not same. Thus, the *different* enharmonic interval (i.e., not the same as double-augmented fifth) would be B and C—wait, question: \"Which is a **different** enharmonic interval from the double-augmented fifth?\" So which option does *not* equal 9 semitones. Double-augmented fifth = 9 semitones (major sixth). A = major sixth → same. B = diminished seventh = 8 semitones → different. C = double-augmented fourth = 7 semitones → different. D = “Major sixth and diminished seventh” → includes both same and different. Since D lists two, and one (diminished seventh) is different, but the phrasing implies the pair— ambiguous. But best answer is B, since it’s definitively different and uniquely listed. So boxed B.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
