      "generated_answer": "Let’s check each statement:  \n\n① **False** — C major (C–E–G) and C natural minor (C–E♭–G) share only one common chord: the tonic triad itself (C–E♭–G is not in C major, so they share only C major’s tonic? Actually, C major’s chords: C, Dm, Em, F, G, Am, B°; C natural minor’s chords: Cm, D°, E♭, Fm, Gm, A♭, B♭. Only E♭ major and A♭ major appear in neither C major, so no two common triads except possibly none? Wait, check: C major’s F major (F–A–C) is not in C minor’s set; C minor’s E♭ (E♭–G–B♭) not in C major. Actually, they share zero common triads because relative major of C minor is E♭ major, not C major. So statement says “2 common chords” — false.**  \n\n② **True** — Neapolitan sixth in C major is D♭–F–A♭ (second degree lowered, first inversion), in C minor is also D♭–F–A♭. Same structure, same inversion.  \n\n③ **False** — “Chords adjacent to Neapolitan sixth” — typically N6 resolves to V or to cadential ⁶₄; intervals description “minor third and perfect fourth” seems arbitrary and not a standard rule for adjacent chords.  \n\nOnly ② is correct → **1 true statement**.  \n\n\\boxed{B}",
