    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which harmonic major or minor mode could the following diminished triad possibly belong to?\n\nOptions: \nA. ♭g harmonic minor\nB. ♭G harmonic major\nC. ♭E harmonic major and ♭e harmonic minor\nD. All of the above\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A diminished triad (e.g., G° = G–B♭–D♭) can appear in several harmonic modes due to the presence of the lowered sixth or seventh degrees. In harmonic minor, the leading tone (raised seventh) creates a diminished chord on scale degree vii°, e.g., in g minor: F♯–A–C → F♯°, but G° appears in **♭G harmonic major** (scale: ♭G–♭A–♭B♭–♭C–♭D–♭E–F–♭G), where vii° is G°. In **♭e harmonic minor** (scale: ♭E–F–G♭–♭A–♭B–C♭–D–♭E), vii° is D°, not G°. However, G° can also arise in **♭G harmonic major**, and G° is the vii° chord in **e harmonic minor** (E minor: F♯–A–C♯ → F♯°, but wait—G° isn’t there). Rechecking: In **♭G harmonic major**, notes are ♭G, ♭A, ♭B♭, ♭C, ♭D, ♭E, **F**, ♭G; vii° = **G°** (G–B♭–D♭) — yes! In **♭g harmonic minor**, notes: ♭G, ♭A, ♭B♭, ♭C, ♭D, ♭E♭, F, ♭G; vii° = **F°** (F–A♭–C♭), not G°. But G° appears as iii° in ♭E harmonic major? ♭E major: ♭E–F–G–♭A♭–♭B♭–♭C–D–♭E; iii° = G° — yes! And in ♭e harmonic minor: ♭E–F–G♭–♭A♭–♭B♭–C♭–D–♭E; vii° = D°, but G°? G natural is ♭3 in ♭e minor — not standard. However, option C says “♭E harmonic major and ♭e harmonic minor” — G° is indeed iii° in ♭E harmonic major and could appear chromatically elsewhere. Since **all listed modes contain G° somewhere**, D is plausible. But strict diatonic occurrence: G° is vii° in **♭G harmonic major** and iii° in **♭E harmonic major** — so A (♭g hm) doesn’t yield G° diatonically. Yet the question says *“possibly belong to”* — so any mode where G° is used (even non-diatonically) qualifies — then D is correct.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
