    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_245",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Among the following chords, how many chords can be considered as enharmonically equivalent to the predominant III in C minor? ①Subdominant in A-flat minor (6-4) ② Dominant seventh in A minor ③ Predominant III in E minor (^6_4) ④ Dominant #5-6 in E major\n\nOptions: \nA. 0\nB. 1\nC. 2\nD. 3\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In C minor, the predominant III chord is the Neapolitan sixth, built on the lowered scale degree 2 (D♭), i.e., D♭–F–A♭ in first inversion (F–A♭–D♭).  \n① Subdominant in A-flat minor (6-4): A♭ minor’s subdominant is D♭, so D♭–F–A♭ in 6⁴ = F–A♭–D♭ → matches Neapolitan of C minor.  \n② Dominant seventh in A minor: E–G♯–B–D ≠ any form of D♭–F–A♭.  \n③ Predominant III in E minor (^6_4): E minor’s ^6 is C♯; C♯–E–G♯ in 6⁴ = E–G♯–C♯ ≠ D♭–F–A♭.  \n④ Dominant #5-6 in E major: E major dominant is B, with raised 5th = B–D♯–F♯–A; #5-6 inversion adds accidentals inconsistent with D♭–F–A♭.  \nOnly ① matches.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
