    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following descriptions about pitch range is incorrect?\n\nOptions: \nA. Unaccented octave, capital letter group, and small letter one group belong to the middle pitch range\nB. Small letter group, small letter one group, and small letter two group belong to the middle pitch range\nC. Small letter three group, small letter four group, and small letter five group belong to the high pitch range\nD. Capital letter group, capital letter one group, and capital letter two group belong to the low pitch range\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In standard music notation, pitch ranges are categorized as follows:  \n- Low pitch range includes the great octave (capital letters G, F, E, D, C) and small octave (lowercase c, d, e), but **not** capital letter groups beyond C (e.g., C, D, E in uppercase typically refer to lower registers in some systems, but \"capital letter group\" usually means C₁–B₁, i.e., great octave).  \nOption A incorrectly includes the *unaccented octave* (通常指小字一组, i.e., small letter group) alongside capital and small letter one groups as middle pitch—actually, unaccented octave (c₁–b₁) is part of the middle range, and small letter one group is the same; however, the capital letter group (C₂–B₂?) is ambiguous, but traditionally capital letters denote lower octaves.  \nBut critically, **Option B** is incorrect because it lumps small letter group (c–b), small letter one group (c₁–b₁), and small letter two group (c₂–b₂) into the *middle* pitch range—yet small letter two group is generally considered *high* range.  \nThus, **B is the most clearly incorrect statement**.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
