    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_354",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following modes are not related by the same tonic?\n\nOptions: \nA. ♭E Ya-yue Shang mode\nB. Qing-yue Yu mode with ♭G as the Gong note\nC. Yan-yue Zhi mode with ♭G as the Run note\nD. The pentatonic mode with G as the Jue note\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In traditional Chinese modal theory, the tonic (Gong) anchors the mode; modes sharing the same tonic belong to the same “key.”  \nOption A: ♭E Ya-yue Shang → tonic is ♭E.  \nOption B: Qing-yue Yu with ♭G as Gong → tonic is ♭G.  \nOption C: Yan-yue Zhi with ♭G as Run (i.e., fifth degree), so Gong = ♭C (since Run = G solfège = ♭G implies Gong = ♭C), but Yan-yue uses different pitch standards—still, tonic is ♭C.  \nOption D: Pentatonic with G as Jue (third degree), so tonic (Gong) is E♭ (since Jue = 3rd, and in Chinese system, E♭–F–G–A–C ⇒ Gong = E♭). Thus D also has tonic ♭E. So A and D share tonic ♭E; B and C differ. Therefore, **B** and **C** are not on the same tonic as A/D, but the question asks for the *one* that is *not* related by the same tonic—likely meaning the outlier among options sharing a common tonic. However, only A and D share ♭E; B and C each have different tonics (♭G and ♭C). Since the question expects one answer, and **C** uses Yan-yue (Tang-style), where Run = ♭G implies a different scale structure (e.g., ♭C–♭D–♭E–F–♭G), tonic is ♭C, unlike A/D’s ♭E and B’s ♭G. Yet **C** is most distinct due to Yan-yue’s unique tuning and non-Heptatonic alignment—thus **C** is least related.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
