    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4698",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nThe passage below is the Chinese emperor's response to English King George III's diplomatic envoys, who were seeking expanded trading privileges (1793).\nStrange and costly objects do not interest me. If I have commanded that the tribute offerings sent by you, O King, are to be accepted, this was solely in consideration for the spirit which prompted you to dispatch them from afar. . . . As your Ambassador can see for himself, we possess all things. I set no value on objects strange or ingenious, and have no use for your country's manufactures. It behooves you, O King, to display even greater devotion and loyalty in future, so that, by perpetual submission to our Throne, you may secure peace and prosperity.\nWhy were the Chinese not interested in expanding trading rights with Great Britain?\n\nOptions: \nA. They were afraid that the British would gain too much influence within China.\nB. They had a religious belief against trading with foreign nations.\nC. They had no interest in the products that Great Britain could provide.\nD. They were preparing for war with Great Britain.\nE. They believed that Great Britain was trying to trick them.\nF. The Chinese were planning to start their own manufacturing industry.\nG. The Chinese saw the British goods as inferior in quality.\nH. The Chinese had a preexisting exclusive trade agreement with the Dutch.\nI. The Chinese were angry over Britain's interference in their foreign affairs.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The passage indicates that the Chinese emperor viewed foreign goods as unnecessary and unimpressive, stating he \"set no value on objects strange or ingenious\" and had \"no use for your country's manufactures.\" This reflects a perception of self-sufficiency and superiority rather than fear, religious doctrine, or political agreements. Therefore, the most accurate answer is that they had no interest in British products.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 403,
        "output_tokens": 84,
        "total_tokens": 487
      },
      "provider": "local-vllm",
