      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}  \nThe secondarily altricial condition in humans evolved because bipedalism narrowed the female pelvis, limiting the size of the baby’s head at birth. This trade-off allowed for continued postnatal brain growth, supporting the large brains seen in modern humans. Thus, the brain could grow significantly after birth despite the constraints of the birth canal.",
