y_1 = 5. Therefore, print No.\n- For the second question, the graph G^{{(2)}} is not good because it has a path 2 \\rightarrow 6 connecting vertices x_2 = 2 and y_2 = 6. Therefore, print No.\n- For the third question, the graph G^{{(3)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n- For the fourth question, the graph G^{{(4)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n\nAs seen in this sample input, note that the given graph G may have self-loops or multi-edges.",
    "prediction": "gpt-4o-mini",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem, we will use the Disjoint Set Union (DSU) or Union-Find data structure to efficiently keep track of the connected components in the graph. Given the nature of the graph and the input conditions, we can check the validity of adding edges in real-time without needing to reconstruct the whole graph for each query.\n\nThe approach can be broken down as follows:\n\n1. **Union-Find Structure**: We'll implement the disjoint set to manage connected components. This will help us determine if two vertices are part of the same component after adding each queried edge.\n\n2. **Initial Graph Representation**: We'll build the initial graph using the edges provided, while also checking that the graph remains \"good\" with respect to the given pairs `(x_i, y_i)`.\n\n3. **Processing Queries**: For each query, we'll check if adding the edge `(p_i, q_i)` keeps the graph \"good\". To be good, we need to ensure none of the specified pairs `(x_j, y_j)` become connected.\n\n4. **Output Results**: After checking each query, we will output \"Yes\" or \"No\" based on whether adding the respective edge still maintains the graph's goodness.\n\nHere's the complete Python function implementing this logic:\n\n```python\ndef find(parent, x):\n    if parent[x] != x:\n        parent[x] = find(parent, parent[x])\n    return parent[x]\n\ndef union(parent, rank, x, y):\n    rootX = find(parent, x)\n    rootY = find(parent, y)\n    \n    if rootX != rootY:\n        if rank[rootX] > rank[rootY]:\n            parent[rootY] = rootX\n        elif rank[rootX] < rank[rootY]:\n            parent[rootX] = rootY\n        else:\n            parent[rootY] = rootX\n            rank[rootX] += 1\n\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().splitlines()\n    \n    idx = 0\n    N, M = map(int, data[idx].split())\n    idx += 1\n    \n    parent = list(range(N + 1))\n    rank = [0] * (N + 1)\n\n    # Read initial edges and union them\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u, v = map(int, data[idx].split())\n        union(parent, rank, u, v)\n        idx += 1\n    \n    K = int(data[idx])\n    idx += 1\n    \n    # Store the pairs (x_i, y_i) and their roots\n    forbidden_pairs = []\n    for _ in range(K):\n        x, y = map(int, data[idx].split())\n        forbidden_pairs.append((x, y))\n        idx += 1\n\n    Q = int(data[idx])\n    idx += 1\n    \n    output = []\n\n    # Process each query\n    for _ in range(Q):\n        p, q = map(int, data[idx].split())\n        idx += 1\n\n        # Check if adding edge p-q connects any forbidden pairs\n        any_connected = False\n        \n        union(parent, rank, p, q)  # Simulate adding the edge\n        \n        for x, y in forbidden_pairs:\n            if find(parent, x) == find(parent, y):\n                any_connected = True\n                break\n        \n        # Revert the union\n        parent_backup = parent[:]\n        union(parent_backup, rank, p, q)\n\n        output.append(\"No\" if any_connected else \"Yes\")\n        \n        # Restore parent array to not mix effects of unions\n        parent = parent_backup\n\n    print(\"\\n\".join(output))\n\n# This call is required to allow the function to run when the script is executed\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- The `find` function is used to find the root of a component.\n- The `union` function merges two components.\n- We initially process the edges and set up our union-find data structure to reflect connections in the graph.\n- We then check each query by temporarily adding the edge and checking against the forbidden pairs. After checking, we need to revert the union operation before examining the next query to ensure state integrity. \n- Finally, the results for each query are collected and printed in one go to maintain performance with large input sizes. \n\nThis solution should efficiently handle the constraints provided in the prompt.",
