      "generated_answer": "Here's the Python function that takes the input from stdin and prints the output:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef soln():\n    N, M = map(int, input().split())\n    graph = defaultdict(list)\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u, v = map(int, input().split())\n        graph[u].append(v)\n        graph[v].append(u)\n    \n    K = int(input())\n    bad_pairs = set()\n    for _ in range(K):\n        x, y = map(int, input().split())\n        bad_pairs.add((x, y))\n        bad_pairs.add((y, x))\n    \n    Q = int(input())\n    for _ in range(Q):\n        p, q = map(int, input().split())\n        if (p, q) in bad_pairs or (q, p) in bad_pairs:\n            print(\"No\")\n        else:\n            if p in graph and q in graph:\n                for neighbor in graph[p]:\n                    if neighbor in graph[q]:\n                        print(\"No\")\n                        break\n                else:\n                    print(\"Yes\")\n            else:\n                print(\"Yes\")\n\nsoln()\n```\n\nTo use this function, simply call `soln()` after the definition.\n\nThe function first reads the input from stdin, creating a graph represented as a defaultdict, and a set of bad pairs of vertices that should not be connected. Then, for each query, it checks if the new edge would create a path between any of the bad pairs. If so, it prints \"No\", otherwise it prints \"Yes\".",
