      "generated_answer": "Here's the Python function that solves the problem:\n\n```python\nimport heapq\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solve():\n    N, M = map(int, input().split())\n    graph = defaultdict(list)\n    total_weight = 0\n\n    for _ in range(M):\n        K, C = map(int, input().split())\n        vertices = list(map(int, input().split()))\n        for i in range(K):\n            for j in range(i+1, K):\n                u, v = vertices[i], vertices[j]\n                graph[u].append((v, C))\n                graph[v].append((u, C))\n                total_weight += C\n\n    visited = [False] * (N+1)\n    pq = [(0, 1)]\n    mst_weight = 0\n\n    while pq:\n        weight, u = heapq.heappop(pq)\n        if not visited[u]:\n            visited[u] = True\n            mst_weight += weight\n\n            for v, c in graph[u]:\n                if not visited[v]:\n                    heapq.heappush(pq, (c, v))\n\n    if any(not visited[i] for i in range(1, N+1)):\n        print(-1)\n    else:\n        print(mst_weight)\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nThis function uses Kruskal's algorithm to find the minimum spanning tree of the graph. It first constructs the graph by adding the edges specified in the input. Then, it uses a priority queue to perform a Kruskal-like algorithm, adding the edges to the minimum spanning tree in order of increasing weight. Finally, it checks if the graph is connected and prints the total weight of the minimum spanning tree or -1 if the graph is not connected.",
