wn: (2,1)=.\n   - left: (1,0)=# → comp0\n   → neighbors: comp0, comp1 → k=2\n   → new comp = 2+1-2 = 1 ✅\n\n3. (2,1): neighbors:\n   - up: (1,1)=.\n   - down: out\n   - left: (2,0)=# → comp0\n   - right: (2,2)=.\n   → only comp0 → k=1\n   → new comp = 2+1-1 = 2 ✅\n\n4. (2,2): neighbors:\n   - up: (1,2)=# → comp1\n   - left: (2,1)=.\n   → only comp1 → k=1\n   → new comp = 2+1-1 = 2 ✅\n\nSo sum of new components: 1+1+2+2 = 6 → expected = 6/4 = 3/2\n\nNow compute $ 3 \\cdot 2^{-1} \\mod 998244353 $\n\n$ 2^{-1} \\mod 998244353 = (998244353+1)//2 = 499122177 $? Wait:\n\nActually: $ 2 \\cdot 499122177 = 998244354 \\equiv 1 \\mod 998244353 $\n\nSo $ 3/2 \\mod 998244353 = 3 \\cdot 499122177 \\mod 998244353 = 1497366531 \\mod 998244353 $\n\nBut 1497366531 > 998244353 → 1497366531 - 998244353 = 499122178\n\nSo output = 499122178 → matches sample.\n\nBut in our formula:\n\n$ \\mathbb{E} = \\frac{(C+1)*R - \\text{total\\_k}}{R} = \\frac{(2+1)*4 - (2+2+1+1)}{4} = \\frac{12 - 6}{4} = \\frac{6}{4} = \\frac{3}{2} $\n\nYes.\n\nSo in code, we had:\n- C = 2\n- R = 4\n- total_k = 2+2+1+1 = 6\n- numerator = (3 * 4 - 6) = 6\n- ans = 6 * inv(4) mod MOD\n\nWait! But earlier we said $ \\mathbb{E} = C+1 - \\text{avg}(k) = 3 - 6/4 = 6/4 $? No:\n\nWait: $ \\mathbb{E} = \\frac{1}{R} \\sum (C+1 - k) = \\frac{1}{4} ( (3-2)+(3-2)+(3-1)+(3-1) ) = \\frac{1+1+2+2}{4} = 6/4 $\n\nBut $ (C+1)*R - total_k = 3*4 - 6 = 6 $, so $ \\mathbb{E} = 6 / 4 = 3/2 $\n\nYes.\n\nBut 6/4 reduces to 3/2, but we can still compute $ 6 \\cdot \\text{inv}(4) \\mod MOD $\n\n$ \\text{inv}(4) = 748683265 $? Let's check: $ 4 \\cdot 748683265 = 2994733060 $, mod $ 998244353 $?\n\nActually, better to compute:\n\nWe can use: $ \\text{inv}(4) = \\text{inv}(2^2) = (499122177)^2 \\mod MOD $? No, just use pow(4, MOD-2, MOD)\n\nBut we don’t need to reduce fraction — just compute $ 6 \\cdot \\text{inv}(4) \\mod MOD $\n\nBut 6/4 = 3/2 → same as $ 3 \\cdot \\text{inv}(2) \\mod MOD = 3 * 499122177 = 1497366531 \\mod MOD = 1497366531 % 9982