      "generated_answer": "Here's the executable Python function that takes stdin as input and prints the output:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef soln():\n    H, W = map(int, input().split())\n    grid = [list(input()) for _ in range(H)]\n    \n    def bfs(i, j):\n        queue = deque([(i, j)])\n        grid[i][j] = '.'\n        count = 1\n        while queue:\n            x, y = queue.popleft()\n            for dx, dy in [(1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)]:\n                nx, ny = x + dx, y + dy\n                if 0 <= nx < H and 0 <= ny < W and grid[nx][ny] == '#':\n                    grid[nx][ny] = '.'\n                    queue.append((nx, ny))\n                    count += 1\n        return count\n    \n    green_sizes = []\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '#':\n                green_sizes.append(bfs(i, j))\n    \n    mod = 998244353\n    total_expected = 0\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                new_green_sizes = green_sizes + [bfs(i, j)]\n                total_expected += sum(new_green_sizes) / len(new_green_sizes)\n                total_expected %= mod\n    \n    print(int(total_expected))\n\nsoln()\n```\n\nTo use this function, simply call `soln()` after the definition.\n\nThe function first reads the input and creates a grid representation of the given data. It then defines a `bfs` function that performs a breadth-first search to count the size of a green connected component.\n\nNext, the function iterates over all the red cells and calculates the expected value of the number of green connected components after repainting each red cell green. It does this by summing the sizes of all the green connected components (including the new one after repainting) and dividing by the total number of red cells.\n\nFinally, the function prints the result modulo 998244353, as specified in the problem statement."