tput 1\n\n3\r\n1 1 1 0 0 1 0 1\r\n\nThe sample output corresponds to the following actions:\n\n- Find potions of types 2,3,1 in this order. Pick up all of them.\n- Find potions of types 3,2 in this order. Do not pick up any of them.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Pick it up.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Do not pick it up.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Pick it up.\n- Encounter a type-2 monster. Use one type-2 potion to defeat it.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Encounter a type-1 monster. Use one type-1 potion to defeat it.\n\nIn this sequence of actions, the value of K is 3.\nThere is no way to avoid defeat with K\\leq 2, so the sought value of K _ {{\\min}} is 3.\r\nThere are multiple sequences of actions that satisfy K=3 and allow him to avoid defeat; you may print any of them.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n4\r\n2 3\r\n1 4\r\n2 1\r\n1 2\n\nSample Output 2\n\n-1\r\n\nHe will inevitably be defeated by the first monster he encounters.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n30\r\n1 25\r\n1 2\r\n1 10\r\n1 18\r\n2 18\r\n1 11\r\n2 11\r\n1 21\r\n1 6\r\n2 2\r\n2 10\r\n1 11\r\n1 24\r\n1 11\r\n1 3\r\n1 2\r\n1 18\r\n2 25\r\n1 8\r\n1 10\r\n1 11\r\n2 18\r\n2 10\r\n1 10\r\n2 2\r\n1 24\r\n1 10\r\n2 10\r\n1 25\r\n2 6\n\nSample Output 3\n\n4\r\n1 1 1 1 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 1 1 0 1 0 1 0 0 0",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\n\ndef solve_adventure(N, events):\n    # events: list of (t, x) for i = 0..N-1\n    # Step 0: check feasibility and compute minimal K\n    INF = 10**9\n    # For each monster event, we need a potion of the same type from some previous pick-up.\n    # Greedy from the end: for each type, maintain a stack of indices of type-1 events (potion finds)\n    # that are \"available\" to be used for later monsters.\n    potion_stack = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]  # type -> list of indices (0-indexed)\n    # We'll also need to track which potions we actually pick.\n    pick = [0] * N  # only for t_i == 1, will be set to 0/1\n    # First, decide for each type-1 event whether picking is necessary.\n    # Work backwards: for each monster, we need to assign a preceding pick-up.\n    need = [0] * N  # for type-1 events: 1 if we must pick, else 0\n    possible = True\n    for i in range(N - 1, -1, -1):\n        t, x = events[i]\n        if t == 1:\n            # potion find: push index onto stack for this type\n            potion_stack[x].append(i)\n        else:  # t == 2, monster\n            if potion_stack[x]:\n                idx = potion_stack[x].pop()   # most recent potion find before this monster\n                need[idx] = 1                 # we must pick that potion\n            else:\n                possible = False\n                break\n    if not possible:\n        return -1\n    # Now we know which type-1 events must be picked (need[i] == 1).\n    # For the remaining type-1 events (need[i]==0), we are free to pick or not to minimize max inventory.\n    # We need to compute minimal possible K_max.\n    # We will simulate from left to right with a greedy that delays picking optional potions as much as possible.\n    # But we must also respect that we need to have potions when monsters appear.\n    # Since mandatory picks are forced, we can decide optional picks to keep inventory low.\n    # Strategy: for each type, keep a queue of mandatory picks that are \"waiting\" for future monsters.\n    # Actually, we can think of the process:\n    # We have mandatory picks (need[i]==1). They are already decided.\n    # The optional picks (need[i]==0) we can skip entirely to keep inventory minimal.\n    # However, we must verify that if we skip all optional picks, we never run out of potions for monsters.\n    # But mandatory picks already ensure each monster gets a potion. Skipping optional picks cannot cause a deficit,\n    # because optional picks are not required by any monster. So we can set pick[i] = need[i] for all type-1.\n    # Then the inventory will be at most the number of mandatory picks held simultaneously.\n    # But is that always minimal? Could picking an optional potion and then using it later reduce the maximum?\n    # No, picking extra potion can only increase inventory, never decrease. So optimal is to never pick unnecessary potions.\n    # Therefore, K_min = max over time of number of mandatory picks that are currently held.\n    # We need to compute that maximum.\n    # We'll simulate with inventory counter per type? Actually total inventory.\n    inventory = 0\n    max_inv = 0\n    active = [0] * (N + 1)  # number of potions of each type currently held\n    # We also need the sequence of picks (0/1) for type-1 events in order.\n    pick_seq = [0] * N\n    for i in range(N):\n        t, x = events[i]\n        if t == 1:\n            if need[i]:\n                pick_seq[i] = 1\n                inventory += 1\n                active[x] += 1\n            else:\n                pick_seq[i] = 0   # skip\n        else:  # monster\n            # must use one potion of type x\n            active[x] -= 1\n            inventory -= 1\n        if inventory > max_inv:\n            max_inv = inventory\n    return max_inv, [pick_seq[i] for i in range(N) if events[i][0] == 1]\n\n```",
