      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef countSubMultisets(nums, l, r):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    counts = Counter(nums)\n    \n    # dp[s] will store the number of sub-multisets with sum s\n    # We treat zeros separately: they can be included in any valid sub-multiset\n    zero_count = counts.pop(0, 0)\n    \n    dp = [0] * (r + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1\n    \n    for val, count in counts.items():\n        new_dp = list(dp)\n        # Use sliding window sum optimization for the knapsack problem\n        # dp[i] = sum(dp[i - val * k]) for k from 0 to count\n        # This is equivalent to dp[i] = dp[i] + dp[i-val] + ... + dp[i - val*count]\n        # Which can be maintained as a running sum\n        for rem in range(val):\n            window_sum = 0\n            for i in range(rem, r + 1, val):\n                window_sum = (window_sum + dp[i]) % MOD\n                if i >= val * (count + 1):\n                    window_sum = (window_sum - dp[i - val * (count + 1)] + MOD) % MOD\n                new_dp[i] = window_sum\n        dp = new_dp\n        \n    # The sum range [l, r]\n    # Include the zero_count: each zero can either be included or not.\n    # Total ways to include zeros is (zero_count + 1)\n    result = 0\n    for s in range(l, r + 1):\n        result = (result + dp[s]) % MOD\n        \n    return (result * (zero_count + 1)) % MOD\n```",
