      "generated_answer": "Here's the Python function that solves the problem:\n\n```python\ndef countSubMultisets(nums, l, r):\n    n = len(nums)\n    mod = 10 ** 9 + 7\n\n    # Count the frequency of each element in the array\n    freq = [0] * (2 * 10 ** 4 + 1)\n    for num in nums:\n        freq[num] += 1\n\n    # Dynamic programming to count the number of sub-multisets\n    dp = [[0] * (r + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    dp[0][0] = 1\n\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        for j in range(r + 1):\n            dp[i][j] = (dp[i - 1][j] + dp[i - 1][max(j - nums[i - 1], 0)] * freq[nums[i - 1]]) % mod\n\n    return sum(dp[n][l:r + 1]) % mod\n```\n\nThe function `countSubMultisets` takes three arguments:\n\n1. `nums`: the input array of non-negative integers.\n2. `l`: the lower bound of the inclusive range.\n3. `r`: the upper bound of the inclusive range.\n\nThe function returns the count of sub-multisets within `nums` where the sum of elements in each subset falls within the inclusive range of `[l, r]`, modulo `10^9 + 7`.\n\nThe main steps of the solution are:\n\n1. Count the frequency of each element in the array.\n2. Use dynamic programming to compute the number of sub-multisets for each sum from `0` to `r`.\n3. Sum the number of sub-multisets with sums between `l` and `r` to get the final result.\n\nNote that the function body is returned without invoking it.",
