      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we observe the effect of the bitwise operation: `(nums[i] AND nums[j])` and `(nums[i] OR nums[j])`. When we perform this operation, the total number of set bits (1s) at each bit position across all numbers remains constant. \n\nBy repeatedly applying the operation, we can effectively \"move\" the set bits such that if a bit position is set in some numbers, we can aggregate those bits into as few numbers as possible to maximize the sum of squares. Specifically, for each bit position from 0 to 30 (since $nums[i] \\le 10^9$), we count how many numbers in the array have that bit set. If a bit is set in $c$ numbers, we can ensure that this bit is set in the \"top\" $c$ numbers of our array.\n\nAfter determining the final values of all numbers, we sort them in descending order, take the top $k$, and compute the sum of their squares modulo $10^9 + 7$.\n\n```python\ndef maxSumOfSquares(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    # There are at most 30 bits for values up to 10^9\n    bit_counts = [0] * 31\n    for x in nums:\n        for i in range(31):\n            if (x >> i) & 1:\n                bit_counts[i] += 1\n    \n    # Reconstruct the k largest possible numbers\n    # Each bit position i can be distributed to the top bit_counts[i] numbers\n    res_nums = [0] * len(nums)\n    for i in range(31):\n        for j in range(bit_counts[i]):\n            res_nums[j] |= (1 << i)\n            \n    # Sort to get the k largest\n    res_nums.sort(reverse=True)\n    \n    total_sum_sq = 0\n    for i in range(k):\n        total_sum_sq = (total_sum_sq + res_nums[i]**2) % MOD\n        \n    return total_sum_sq\n```",
