      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem of calculating the area covered by multiple overlapping rectangular sheets on a coordinate plane, we can use the technique of coordinate compression and active intervals to keep track of the covered regions. Here's the complete solution in the form of an executable Python function:\n\n```python\ndef calculate_covered_area():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().splitlines()\n    \n    N = int(data[0])\n    rectangles = [list(map(int, line.split())) for line in data[1:N+1]]\n    \n    # Collecting all unique y-coordinates for compression\n    y_coords = set()\n    for _, _, c, d in rectangles:\n        y_coords.add(c)\n        y_coords.add(d)\n    \n    y_coords = sorted(y_coords)\n    y_mapping = {y: i for i, y in enumerate(y_coords)}\n    \n    # Coordinate compression\n    event_count = len(y_coords)\n    height_count = [0] * (event_count - 1)\n    \n    for a, b, c, d in rectangles:\n        height_count[y_mapping[c]] += 1\n        height_count[y_mapping[d]] -= 1\n    \n    # At this point, we will sweep the x-axis\n    covered_area = 0\n    prev_y = -1\n    active_intervals = 0\n    \n    for sheet in rectangles:\n        a, b, c, d = sheet\n        \n        # Process the covered y-intervals\n        # we must keep track of how many active intervals we have\n        for i in range(a, b):\n            active_intervals += height_count[y_mapping[c]]\n            active_intervals -= height_count[y_mapping[d]]\n            if prev_y != -1:\n                # Calculate the height contributed by the active y-intervals\n                covered_height = sum(1 for h in height_count if h > 0)\n                covered_area += covered_height * (i - prev_y)\n            prev_y = i\n            \n        # Reset for next rectangle\n        prev_y = -1\n        height_count = [0] * (event_count - 1)  # Reset after processing each rectangle\n\n    print(covered_area)\n\ncalculate_covered_area()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n1. **Input Handling**: The function reads input, splitting it into lines. The first line contains the number of rectangles, and the following lines contain the rectangle's coordinates.\n\n2. **Coordinate Compression**: The y-coordinates of the rectangles are extracted and sorted. This allows us to map the original y-coordinates into a compressed range, which makes it easier to manage overlaps.\n\n3. **Event Counting**: The event counting technique keeps track of how many rectangles are currently \"active\" at any given y-coordinate. This necessitates an increment when entering a rectangle and a decrement when exiting.\n\n4. **Calculating Areas**: The main sweep occurs horizontally across the x-coordinates of the rectangles. For every x-coordinate range, the function calculates the covered height and computes the area contributed by that range.\n\n5. **Output**: Finally, after processing all rectangles, the computed area is printed.\n\n### Usage:\nYou can run this function in a Python environment where you can provide standard input (stdin), or adapt it to take input directly in a different format if required. The function handles the calculations based on the constraints given in the problem.",
