  {
    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_405",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a grid of H rows and W columns, each cell having a side length of 1, and we have N tiles.\nThe i-th tile (1\\leq i\\leq N) is a rectangle of size A_i\\times B_i.\nDetermine whether it is possible to place the tiles on the grid so that all of the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- Every cell is covered by exactly one tile.\n- It is fine to have unused tiles.\n- The tiles may be rotated or flipped when placed. However, each tile must be aligned with the edges of the cells without extending outside the grid.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN H W\nA_1 B_1\nA_2 B_2\n\\ldots\nA_N B_N\n\nOutput\n\nIf it is possible to place the tiles on the grid so that all of the conditions in the problem statement are satisfied, print Yes; otherwise, print No.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq N\\leq 7\n- 1 \\leq H,W \\leq 10\n- 1\\leq A_i,B_i\\leq 10\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n5 5 5\n1 1\n3 3\n4 4\n2 3\n2 5\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\n\nPlacing the 2-nd, 4-th, and 5-th tiles as shown below covers every cell of the grid by exactly one tile.\n\nHence, print Yes.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n1 1 2\n2 3\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\n\nIt is impossible to place the tile without letting it extend outside the grid.\nHence, print No.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n1 2 2\n1 1\n\nSample Output 3\n\nNo\n\nIt is impossible to cover all cells with the tile.\nHence, print No.\n\nSample Input 4\n\n5 3 3\n1 1\n2 2\n2 2\n2 2\n2 2\n\nSample Output 4\n\nNo\n\nNote that each cell must be covered by exactly one tile.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    from itertools import permutations\n    \n    # Read input\n    lines = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    if not lines:\n        print(\"No\")\n        return\n        \n    n, h, w = map(int, lines[0].split())\n    tiles = []\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        a, b = map(int, lines[i].split())\n        tiles.append((a, b))\n    \n    # Total area of the grid\n    grid_area = h * w\n    \n    # Try all possible subsets of tiles and their permutations\n    # Since N <= 7, we can try all 2^N subsets\n    from itertools import combinations\n    \n    # Check if we can fill the grid using any subset of tiles\n    for r in range(1, n + 1):\n        for tile_subset in combinations(range(n), r):\n            # Get the tiles in this subset, each can be rotated\n            subset_tiles = [tiles[i] for i in tile_subset]\n            \n            # Calculate total area of selected tiles\n            total_area = sum(a * b for a, b in subset_tiles)\n            \n            # If area doesn't match, skip\n            if total_area != grid_area:\n                continue\n                \n            # Try to place these tiles on the grid\n            # Use backtracking to place rectangles on grid\n            def can_fill_grid():\n                # Create a h x w grid, 0 means empty, 1 means filled\n                grid = [[0 for _ in range(w)] for _ in range(h)]\n                \n                # Get all permutations of the subset tiles\n                for perm in permutations(subset_tiles):\n                    if backtrack(0, grid, perm):\n                        return True\n                return False\n            \n            def backtrack(idx, grid, tile_list):\n                if idx == len(tile_list):\n                    return True  # All tiles placed successfully\n                \n                a, b = tile_list[idx]\n                \n                # Try placing tile without rotation (a x b)\n                for i in range(h):\n                    for j in range(w):\n                        if can_place(grid, i, j, a, b):\n                            place(grid, i, j, a, b, 1)\n                            if backtrack(idx + 1, grid, tile_list):\n                                place(grid, i, j, a, b, 0)  # undo\n                                return True\n                            place(grid, i, j, a, b, 0)  # undo\n                \n                # Try placing tile with rotation (b x a) if different\n                if a != b:\n                    for i in range(h):\n                        for j in range(w):\n                            if can_place(grid, i, j, b, a):\n                                place(grid, i, j, b, a, 1)\n                                if backtrack(idx + 1, grid, tile_list):\n                                    place(grid, i, j, b, a, 0)  # undo\n                                    return True\n                                place(grid, i, j, b, a, 0)  # undo\n                \n                return False\n            \n            def can_place(grid, r, c, height, width):\n                if r + height > h or c + width > w:\n                    return False\n                for i in range(r, r + height):\n                    for j in range(c, c + width):\n                        if grid[i][j] == 1:\n                            return False\n                return True\n            \n            def place(grid, r, c, height, width, val):\n                for i in range(r, r + height):\n                    for j in range(c, c + width):\n                        grid[i][j] = val\n            \n            if can_fill_grid():\n                print(\"Yes\")\n                return\n    \n    print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
