    "prediction": "gpt-4o-mini",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem of determining whether a set of tiles can completely cover a grid according to the specified constraints, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Read Input**: We need to read the dimensions of the grid and the size of each tile.\n2. **Calculate Total Area**: We will compute the total area of the grid and the areas of the tiles, both in their original and rotated orientations.\n3. **Check Feasibility**: We will check if any combination of the tiles can fill the grid exactly without leaving uncovered cells or extending beyond the grid boundaries.\n4. **Backtracking with Pruning**: We can use a recursive backtracking approach to try placing tiles in different configurations while ensuring they are aligned with the grid edges.\n\nHere's the Python code that implements the above logic:\n\n```python\ndef can_cover(grid_h, grid_w, tiles, idx, used_area):\n    if used_area == grid_h * grid_w:\n        return True\n    if idx >= len(tiles) or used_area > grid_h * grid_w:\n        return False\n\n    A, B = tiles[idx]\n    \n    # Try to place the tile in its original orientation\n    for r in range(grid_h - A + 1):\n        for c in range(grid_w - B + 1):\n            # Check if this position can be filled\n            if all(grid[r + dr][c + dc] == 0 for dr in range(A) for dc in range(B)):\n                # Place the tile\n                for dr in range(A):\n                    for dc in range(B):\n                        grid[r + dr][c + dc] = 1\n                \n                # Recur\n                if can_cover(grid_h, grid_w, tiles, idx + 1, used_area + A * B):\n                    return True\n                \n                # Backtrack\n                for dr in range(A):\n                    for dc in range(B):\n                        grid[r + dr][c + dc] = 0\n\n    # Try to place the tile in its rotated orientation\n    A, B = B, A  # Rotate\n    for r in range(grid_h - A + 1):\n        for c in range(grid_w - B + 1):\n            # Check if this position can be filled\n            if all(grid[r + dr][c + dc] == 0 for dr in range(A) for dc in range(B)):\n                # Place the tile\n                for dr in range(A):\n                    for dc in range(B):\n                        grid[r + dr][c + dc] = 1\n\n                # Recur\n                if can_cover(grid_h, grid_w, tiles, idx + 1, used_area + A * B):\n                    return True\n                \n                # Backtrack\n                for dr in range(A):\n                    for dc in range(B):\n                        grid[r + dr][c + dc] = 0\n\n    return False\n\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().strip().splitlines()\n    \n    first_line = list(map(int, data[0].split()))\n    N = first_line[0]\n    H = first_line[1]\n    W = first_line[2]\n    \n    tiles = [tuple(map(int, line.split())) for line in data[1:N+1]]\n    \n    # Prepare an empty grid\n    grid = [[0] * W for _ in range(H)]\n\n    if can_cover(H, W, tiles, 0, 0):\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n### Explanation of the Code:\n- We create a recursive function `can_cover` that checks if we can cover the grid using the available tiles starting from a specific index.\n- For each tile, we try placing it in both its original and rotated orientations.\n- We also use a 2D `grid` array to track which cells are filled.\n- The `main` function coordinates the reading of input and initializes the grid, eventually calling the `can_cover` function.\n\nThis code handles the requirements of determining if the tiles can perfectly cover a grid according to the given rules.",
