      "generated_answer": "Here's the Python function that solves the problem:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve_tile_placement():\n    # Read input\n    line1 = input().split()\n    N, H, W = map(int, line1)\n    tiles = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        line = input().split()\n        A, B = map(int, line)\n        tiles.append((A, B))\n\n    # Check if it's possible to place the tiles\n    for i in range(N):\n        A, B = tiles[i]\n        if A <= H and B <= W:\n            continue\n        if A <= W and B <= H:\n            continue\n        for _ in range(3):\n            A, B = B, A\n            if A <= H and B <= W:\n                continue\n            if A <= W and B <= H:\n                continue\n        return \"No\"\n\n    # All tiles can be placed\n    return \"Yes\"\n\nprint(solve_tile_placement())\n```\n\nYou can call the `solve_tile_placement()` function after the definition to get the output.\n\nHere's how the function works:\n\n1. The input is read from standard input using the `input()` function.\n2. The function checks if it's possible to place each tile on the grid without extending outside the grid. It tries all possible rotations and flips of the tile.\n3. If all tiles can be placed, the function returns \"Yes\"; otherwise, it returns \"No\".\n4. The output is printed using the `print()` function.\n\nNote that this solution assumes that the input is valid and follows the constraints specified in the problem statement.",
